36
00:01:52.284 --> 00:01:57.364
This means that to treat and reorganize this entire space, you will need to

37
00:01:57.364 --> 00:02:02.524
understand that it is necessary to treat and release the diaphragm, because it is what will create

38
00:02:02.524 --> 00:02:05.284
this overall delimitation.

39
00:02:05.544 --> 00:02:10.664
The ascent of my neural tube, the development of

40
00:02:10.664 --> 00:02:15.804
my peritoneal cavity, the establishment of my heart is

41
00:02:15.804 --> 00:02:20.284
a movement that will reintegrate globally towards a focal point.

42
00:02:20.355 --> 00:02:24.755
that we call the umbilical cord. One could say that the umbilical cord,

43
00:02:24.935 --> 00:02:30.415
in its finality, contains the history of this development, of

44
00:02:30.415 --> 00:02:31.755
this unfolding, of

45
00:02:31.755 --> 00:02:36.135
this delimitation, of this reorganization of your peritoneum.

46
00:02:36.355 --> 00:02:42.235
It will integrate into specific rotation movements, organized in

47
00:02:42.235 --> 00:02:42.415
clockwise

48
00:02:42.415 --> 00:02:47.935
and counter-clockwise directions. On a frontal plane, we will see

49
00:02:47.935 --> 00:02:48.055
a

50
00:02:48.426 --> 00:02:53.526
global developmental counter-clockwise movement. On a

51
00:02:53.526 --> 00:02:55.566
transverse plane, we will see a

52
00:02:55.566 --> 00:03:00.886
clockwise developmental movement. This peritoneal cavity will begin to organize,

53
00:03:01.186 --> 00:03:01.986
to delineate.

54
00:03:02.446 --> 00:03:06.426
This means it will move, and in this movement, you must learn to follow this

55
00:03:06.426 --> 00:03:11.466
development. First, there is the peritoneum that surrounds an organ. It’s

56
00:03:11.466 --> 00:03:14.006
called visceral peritoneum.

57
00:03:14.006 --> 00:03:16.126
When a peritoneum...

58
00:03:16.497 --> 00:03:18.517
attaches an organ to the

59
00:03:18.517 --> 00:03:20.217
posterior side, it is called

60
00:03:20.217 --> 00:03:22.437
a mesenterium. If it is the stomach,

61
00:03:22.577 --> 00:03:24.697
it will be called greater omentum.

62
00:03:24.857 --> 00:03:26.617
If it is the duodenum, it will be called

63
00:03:26.617 --> 00:03:28.557
the mesoduodenum. If it is

64
00:03:28.557 --> 00:03:30.637
a colon, it will be called the mesocolon.

65
00:03:30.837 --> 00:03:32.517
Or, depending on

66
00:03:32.517 --> 00:03:34.357
the location, it will be called

67
00:03:34.357 --> 00:03:36.777
a Toldt’s fascia, for example.

68
00:03:37.057 --> 00:03:38.097
But if it’s a

69
00:03:38.717 --> 00:03:40.797
small intestine, it is called

70
00:03:40.797 --> 00:03:42.857
a mesentery.

71
00:03:43.297 --> 00:03:44.437
The peritoneum that has the...

72
00:03:44.568 --> 00:03:50.128
Here, there is the posterior part, which is called the posterior parietal peritoneum.

73
00:03:50.988 --> 00:03:56.068
At the lateral level, it’s called the lateral parietal peritoneum,

74
00:03:56.308 --> 00:03:59.328
the anterior parietal peritoneum.

75
00:03:59.668 --> 00:04:04.728
If it’s a peritoneum that connects one organ to another, it is

76
00:04:04.728 --> 00:04:07.048
called an omentum.

77
00:04:07.248 --> 00:04:12.408
For example, you have the lesser omentum, it is a tissue that connects the liver to the stomach.

78
00:04:12.639 --> 00:04:15.499
You also have an omentum that connects the spleen to the stomach.

79
00:04:15.739 --> 00:04:21.159
An omentum, a meso, is always a double layer that carries

80
00:04:21.159 --> 00:04:23.319
in the middle a vessel.

81
00:04:23.619 --> 00:04:29.299
But in the developmental movement, we will see that there will be folds and a reorganization

82
00:04:29.299 --> 00:04:34.419
to the extent that the omentum here, for example the meso, will be able to come to the side

83
00:04:34.419 --> 00:04:35.079
like this.

84
00:04:42.911 --> 00:04:48.131
And so here, there is a tissue that will attach, and it will form a

85
00:04:48.131 --> 00:04:49.251
adhesion fascia.

86
00:04:49.451 --> 00:04:53.511
This is something that surgeons can easily resect because there are no vessels.

87
00:04:53.771 --> 00:04:56.131
A meso always contains a vessel.

88
00:04:56.231 --> 00:05:00.491
While a ligament or a fascia does not necessarily contain a vessel.

89
00:05:00.591 --> 00:05:06.451
At the level of the peritoneum, we will have the mesoderm, the somatopleure, the splanchnopleure,

90
00:05:06.591 --> 00:05:08.711
the ventral mesogastrium...

91
00:05:09.442 --> 00:05:14.582
and we will have what we call mesos, fascias, omenta that we call

92
00:05:14.582 --> 00:05:17.002
epiplons or ligaments.

93
00:05:17.782 --> 00:05:22.822
All of this is the hyperperitoneum, we can say, initially organized in the peritoneal cavity, I

94
00:05:22.822 --> 00:05:23.062
talk.

95
00:05:24.102 --> 00:05:28.262
There is my intestine that will begin to grow, there is my liver that will develop,

96
00:05:28.422 --> 00:05:32.382
there is my stomach that wants to express itself, there are my lungs that are pushing.

97
00:05:32.582 --> 00:05:34.662
All of this needs to find its place.